---
title: In the Mood for Love
subject: dir. Wong Kar-wai
genre: Film
rating: 5
date: 2026-08-05
excerpt: >-
  Two people almost have an affair for ninety minutes of the most beautifully
  lit almost in film history.
---

Two neighbours discover their spouses are sleeping together, and respond by rehearsing the affair they refuse to have. That is the entire plot, and Wong stretches it across a year of narrow corridors, rain-slicked stairwells and noodle stalls at midnight — all of it shot as though the camera is trying not to be noticed either.

The film's grammar is repetition. The same stairwell, the same slow-motion pass, the same Shigeru Umebayashi waltz until the melody stops being romantic and starts being compulsive. Wong is showing us the shape of a routine that two people build so they have a reason to keep seeing each other.

> He remembers those vanished years as though looking through a dusty window pane.

Maggie Cheung's wardrobe is often written up as decoration; it is closer to a calendar. Twenty-odd cheongsams marking time in a story that otherwise refuses to tell you how much has passed. Tony Leung, meanwhile, gives one of the great performances of withheld feeling — a man whose face is doing the work his dialogue is forbidden from doing.

Then Angkor Wat, a hole in a wall, a secret spoken into stone and sealed with mud. A lesser film would call that tragic. This one seems to understand it as the only dignified option available, which is worse, and far more moving.
